`karr sync` handles `refs/karr/*` only. Helper refs are fetched one at a time by `get-refs`, which is fine for an occasional planning blob and useless for a namespace that carries the chain, the open questions and the run log.

Once coordination state lives under `refs/karr-foundation/*`, that namespace needs the same pull/push treatment the board gets, including the contention retry from #181 and #183.

Open question for the implementer: whether it belongs in `karr sync` itself or in a separate command. Board state and coordination state have different lifetimes — a stale chain matters less than a stale board — but two commands is two things to forget.

Design: `karr get-refs refs/karr-foundation/spec/fleet-execution.md`
Implemented on branch ticket-190-foundation-sync, commit efd9cc3 (local only, not pushed).

DECISION 1 -- karr sync, not a separate command. karr sync is what every failed sync, every runbook and the skill doc already point at; a second command is a second thing to remember and forgetting it fails silently (a chain planning against a board it has not seen, a run log no other machine can read). The ticket's counter-argument about lifetimes is spent where it costs nothing: the implicit sync_before/sync_after every writing command makes stays board-only, so only an explicitly typed karr sync pays for the namespace, and push_foundation returns without a round trip when the clone holds no fleet refs and no unpublished deletion -- which is every repo that is not the hub. Board half runs first, fleet half second, never alone.

DECISION 2 -- deletions travel as tombstones plus explicit delete refspecs (the #178 mechanism), never as a pruning push. The two alternatives both lose data and prune_logs makes it concrete: publishing nothing means every pruned run comes back on the next pull forever; pruning on push takes another machine's run log, written a second ago and never seen by the pusher, off the remote. Git::delete_ref now keys off a table of namespaces whose deletions are published (\@TOMBSTONED) instead of a hardcoded refs/karr/ test. Fleet tombstones live at refs/karr-local/foundation-deleted/ and the mirror at refs/karr-local/foundation-remote/<remote>/ -- both inside refs/karr-local/, the one namespace karr set-refs cannot reach, unlike the board's refs/karr-remote/ (see the ticket I filed, #199).

DECISION 3 -- which board guards carry over. Mirror + the four cases: yes, the namespace meets all four constantly. Wholesale-wipe guard (#82): no -- an emptied chain whose logs aged out is the normal end state, so the guard would fire on the ordinary case, and the only way past it is a --prune that also arms the board's wipe. Board identity (#95): no separate stamp -- the board's runs first, in the same command, against the same remote, which is exactly why the fleet half must never run first or alone. Conflict parking: no -- a displaced card is read back by a person, a displaced chain step is re-planned; the warning stays, the parked ref does not.

No third retry loop: Role::SyncLifecycle now has exactly one (_sync_attempts), shared by sync_pull, sync_after, sync_pull_foundation and sync_push_foundation, so the attempt count, banners, --quiet contract and the #181 contention-versus-refusal verdict live in one place. The four reconciliation cases likewise moved into one _mirror_plan read by both namespaces.

